Z||000004||Archie||03-19-2001||07:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cowans:<BR><B>Do a search on V8 wiring...V8Archie posted an extensive explanation complete with pics. Sandy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Correct <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/v8install.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/v8install.htm</A>  gives you a partial explanation and search PFF for more detailed info I gave when answering a question from someone.<P>BUTTTTTTTTTTTTT,<P>Don't start hacking away at the wiring until you get my Video Instruction Tape.||||reg||1
Z||000005||V8Fiero||03-19-2001||10:21 PM||||Heres my input.<P>If your gonna pull the harness before watching archies tape, use masking tape and label EVERY wire from where it came from. DO NOT cut any wire until you connect all you need AFTER the install.....<P>There...my 2 pennies.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||03-20-2001||07:34 AM||||I agree with V8Fiero,<P>After you pull everything loose, all the connectors can start to look alike. I'm in the process on installing a 89 T.P.I. 350 into a 68 Camaro. Before I removed ANYTHING from the parts car, I bought an inexpensive label machine (white labels w/ black print) and as I disconnected the connector, I labeled what it was or where it came from (ex., "Alt (+)" or "upper throttle body", etc.). <P>I now have the motor in the Camaro and all the connectors re-connected (piece-O-cake)or identified for future connection or elimination. I highly recommend the label machine, the print will not come off if it gets wet, the labels are VERY durible, and the machine is easier to keep track of than a pencil  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> .<P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
